\import{base-macros}

\p{\strong{Status:} implemented. The switches \code{expect}, \code{pchk}, \code{match} and \code{joint} are in the default switch set; \code{tele} is off. The typed bindings (the fix of findings E1-F1 to E1-F6) are in every configuration since 2026-11-24.}

\p{\strong{The judgments.} \code{INFER} computes the type of a term under an environment in one of two modes: \em{check} mode is the official inference that also checks, \em{infer-only} mode trusts the term. \code{CHECK} tests a term against an expected type; in the plain machine it is check-mode inference followed by [conversion](design-conversion). Both are tabled on the suspension record of the term, its pruned environment and its level substitution. A stored type always answers an infer-only query and answers a check-mode query only with a valid check stamp; a type inferred without checking never answers a checking query. A declaration is checked by inferring the sort of its type and checking its value against the value of that type.}

\p{\strong{The four shortcuts.} Each falls through to the generic rule when it cannot answer.}
\table{
\tr{\th{Switch}\th{What it does}\th{Measured on Init}}
\tr{\td{\code{expect}}\td{A lambda is checked against the expected Pi type: its domain is inferred and compared with the expected domain, its body is checked against the instantiated codomain. A lambda argument is checked against its binder's domain.}\td{thread CPU of the check phase 9.58 s to 8.39 s on top of \code{gated}; binder comparisons 0.78 M to 0.48 M (in process, one thread, loaded host, one run: differences below about 10 \% are not established)}}
\tr{\td{\code{pchk}}\td{Shared check facts: a closed export term without a level parameter that passed check mode gets one bit in the store. A later check-mode inference of it, in any declaration on any worker whose roots passed the structural checks, runs infer-only. The bit is never set once a declaration has entered eager reduction.}\td{60,390 bits set, 117,875 inferences answered infer-only, ticks -2.1 \%}}
\tr{\td{\code{match}}\td{The inferred type of an argument is matched against the domain \em{pattern} of the telescope instead of building the domain and comparing: at most 64 pattern nodes, identity up to equivalent levels, never a refutation.}\td{3.19 M domains offered, 2.20 M answered}}
\tr{\td{\code{joint}}\td{While the declared type is a Pi and the value a lambda with the \em{same} domain term, the domain is checked once and one variable is opened for both.}\td{220,353 joint binders, 3.8 per declaration with a value; ticks -1.6 \%}}
}

\p{Together \code{pchk}, \code{match} and \code{joint} take 3.7 \% of the instructions on Init, 4.3 \% on Std, 1.5 \% on the Cedar slice and 0.2 \% on reachability (native image, one worker, on \code{\verb~|warm,gated,expect~}; the host was loaded, so only the instruction counts are exact; CPU time fell by 4.2 \% on Init and 3.3 \% on Std and moved by less than the scatter elsewhere). The gain is small because of where the work is: 59 \% of the value records of Init are made inside comparisons started by argument checks, 88.6 \% of the 11.24 M argument checks compare identical records, and the domains that matching answers were cheap to build.}

\p{\strong{Not kept.} \code{tele}, the code blocks that specialise application inference to a constant, shows no gain on any corpus and loses 2 to 6 \% on the Cedar slice. A direct-mapped front cache for the tabling probe answers 4.13 M of 4.14 M hits on Init and saves no CPU time; it stays off behind a system property.}

\p{\strong{Typed bindings.} The official kernel types a bound variable by its binder's \em{declared} type. The machine binds a variable to a value and types values, so it could use a type that is only convertible with the declared one. That is not harmless, because the official comparison is not transitive: with \code{\verb~|d x := And.rec (fun _ _ => 5) x~} and an axiom \code{h}, the kernel accepts \code{\verb~|d intro = d h~} and \code{\verb~|d intro = 5~} and rejects \code{\verb~|d h = 5~}. Hostile exports built on this showed wrong accepts against the official checker, three of them in the plain machine, within an hour of the machine becoming the default engine (see [that decision](dec-T79V)). The shapes and their fix:}
\ul{
\li{\strong{F1, a let variable} had the type of its value. Now its suspension carries the value of the declared type.}
\li{\strong{F2 and F3, an instantiated lambda type}: the variable had the type of whatever it was bound to, the argument of a redex or the comparison variable of the other side. Now, if the body's type can depend on the type of its variable and the bound value does not have exactly the declared domain as its type, the variable is bound to a \em{typed binding}: a suspension that evaluates to the value and is typed by the domain.}
\li{\strong{F4, \code{expect} with convertible domains}: the rule now applies only when the lambda's domain and the expected domain are one value; otherwise the generic rule runs. The switch \code{convexpect}, off, gives the old rule back.}
\li{\strong{F5}: the expected codomain is no longer stored as the lambda's own codomain.}
\li{\strong{F6, the K rule after an applied let variable} whose declared type is not the type of its value: such a declaration is declined.}
}

\p{The reproducers are rows of the hostile suite, each with the verdict of the official checker. The typed bindings cost nothing measurable; the restriction of \code{expect} is the whole tick cost of the fix (2.4 \% on the Cedar slice, about 0.3 \% on Std, Init and 16 Mathlib slices) and does not show in warm wall clock, see [the decision to keep them](dec-0DSI).}

\p{\strong{What stays inexact (E1-F7).} The official kernel infers the type of a term it has \em{not} reduced in two places: the raw major premise of the K rule, and the structure of a stuck projection. That type goes through the declared domain of an uncontracted redex or the declared type of a let variable; the machine types the value, where the redex is gone. Three hostile exports are still accepted where the official checker rejects; the statement of each is \code{True}, so they are kind T and tolerated by [the exactness contract](design-exactness-contract). An exact treatment would need the type of a suspension to be the inference of its term, suspensions that are never replaced by their known value where they stand for an argument, a stuck projection that keeps the suspension of its structure, and the same in the compiled paths. It was not built. Decline rules instead would cost 322 of 425,620 units of the Mathlib slices and 12 of 100,096 of Std, and would not cover the shapes without a let.}

\p{Related: [the divergence classes](design-official-divergence), [eager reduction](design-eager-reduction), [staging switches](design-switches), [code objects](design-code-objects-and-tiers). Detail: \src{docs/design/ISA.md} sections 4.5 and 5.3, \src{rlvm-machine/NOTES-M5.md} (every deviation, the measurement batches, the sections on E1-F1 to E1-F7), \src{docs/design/review/official-divergence.md} section 1, \src{docs/design/review/soundness-default-set.md} sections 4.2, 4.4 and 4.5. Sources: [the design session](ses-EMMW), [the speed wave](ses-CVTE) and [the exactness session](ses-874D).}

\p{Findings, examples and open problems: [a let whose declared type differs from the type of its value](ex-GZ3D), [typing values against typing unreduced terms](ins-AAXM), [inference is a third of the worker time](prob-QBOP).}
